## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical cache invalidation strategies in multi-region Redis deployments, testing understanding of versioned cache keys, cross-region synchronization, and TTL management. It also assesses knowledge of Redis pub/sub versus keyspace notifications and write-through caching trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Versioned cache keys with TTL management
- Cross-region invalidation patterns
- Redis pub/sub vs keyspace notifications
- Atomic operations and consistency guarantees
- Write-through vs write-behind caching strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: bumpVersion(id)
const newVer = await redis.incr(`product:${id}:ver`);
await redis.publish('cache:invalidate', JSON.stringify({id, ver: newVer}));
```

```javascript
// Pseudo: getCache(id)
const currentVer = await redis.get(`product:${id}:ver`);
const cached = await redis.get(`product:${id}:v${currentVer}`);
if (!cached) {
  // Cache miss - refresh from source
  const data = await fetchFromDatabase(id);
  await redis.setex(`product:${id}:v${currentVer}`, 120, JSON.stringify(data));
  return data;
}
return JSON.parse(cached);
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a multi-IdP, tenant-scoped token flow with replay protection and cross-tenant semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Normalization of tokens across IdPs
- Tenant-scoped metadata cache
- JWT structure and claims
- Replay protection with Redis TTL
- Clock skew handling and cross-tenant semantics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Issuance (pseudocode)
function issueToken(principal, tenant) {
  const jti = crypto.randomUUID();
  const payload = { iss: tenant, sub: principal.sub, aud: 'api', scope: principal.scope, iat: now(), exp: now()+600, jti, nonce: crypto.randomBytes(16).toString('hex'), tenant };
  const token = signJWT(payload, tenantKey);
  redis.set(`nonce:${tenant}:${jti}`, '1', 'EX', 600);
  return token;
}

// Verification (pseudocode)
function verifyToken(token) {
  const payload = verifyJWT(token);
  if (!redis.exists(`nonce:${payload.tenant}:${payload.jti}`)) throw new Error('Replay');
  return payload;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale nonce storage across millions of tenants?
- How would you rotate IdP keys and invalidate old tokens?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design distributed workflow using sagas, CQRS, and event-sourcing in real-world microservices; probes handling of failure modes, idempotency, compensations, and consistency guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Saga orchestration vs choreography
- Event store and projections for CQRS
- Compensating actions and timeouts
- Idempotent handlers and outbox pattern

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: saga orchestrator pattern
class CheckoutSaga {
  async start(order) {
    await dispatch('ReserveInventory', order);
  }
  async onEvent(event) {
    if (event.type === 'InventoryReserved') await dispatch('CapturePayment', event.order);
    else if (event.type === 'PaymentCaptured') await dispatch('ScheduleShipment', event.order);
    else if (event.type === 'ShipmentScheduled') complete(event.order);
    else if (event.type.endsWith('Failed')) compensate(event.order);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- What are the trade-offs between orchestration and choreography in this scenario?
- How would you test the saga under partial failures and timeouts?

